Setting <code>SB_OBJECTSTORE_SSE</code> Key When to use SSE-S3 <code>AES256</code> Managed by S3 Simple setups, S3-compatible servers SSE-KMS <code>aws:kms</code> A KMS key you control (<code>SB_OBJECTSTORE_KMS_KEY_ID</code>) When you need key policies, key rotation control or CloudTrail records of key use <p>Also set the same default encryption on the bucket, so objects written by other tools (for example a restore job) are encrypted too.</p> <p>With SSE-KMS, the api role needs these KMS permissions on the key, in addition to the S3 policy:</p> <ul> <li><code>kms:GenerateDataKey</code> to write objects;</li> <li><code>kms:Decrypt</code> to read them.</li> </ul> <p>Add a statement like this to the key policy, or grant the same actions in the api role's IAM policy if the key policy delegates to IAM:</p> <pre><code>{
  "Sid": "AllowSecretsBridgeApiForS3",
  "Effect": "Allow",
  "Principal": {
    "AWS": "arn:aws:iam::123456789012:role/secrets-bridge-api"
  },
  "Action": [
    "kms:GenerateDataKey",
    "kms:Decrypt"
  ],
  "Resource": "*",
  "Condition": {
    "StringEquals": {
      "kms:ViaService": "s3.us-east-1.amazonaws.com"
    }
  }
}
</code></pre> <p>The <code>kms:ViaService</code> condition limits use of the key to requests made through S3. If you turn on S3 Bucket Keys, the number of KMS calls drops and the policy above still applies.</p> <p>You can use the same KMS key as the <code>aws-kms</code> backend, but a separate key keeps media encryption apart from the key that protects wraps.</p>
